  "narrative": {
    "northStar": "The Investigator's Casebook",
    "overview": "Arkham Table is a record of a case being worked. Between scenarios the player handles the casebook itself: parchment pages with vine borders, set in a blackletter display face and inked in sepia, where the campaign log, the guide's story and the deck choices are written down. During a scenario the casebook is open on a dark table at night. The real card scans, the cardboard tokens and the rulebook art carry the color, and everything the interface adds is a prop on that table: brass fittings, worn leather, a slip of paper with a note.\n\nThe system has two rooms and one material language. The **study** is light: parchment, iron-gall ink, umber gilt. The **table** is dark: night green-black, smoked panels, bone-colored ink and gilt edges. A modal opened over parchment switches back to the table's dark theme, so every dialog reads as an object lifted off the page.\n\nDensity is high on the table, because a hot-seat game needs the whole state in view, and calm in the study, where reading comes first. The HUD is made of props on that table, drawn entirely in CSS: worn brown leather with a fine grain, brass bevels, stitching on the large plates, and a brass rail under the top bar. They have weight and material instead of reading as floating software chrome.\n\nRejected directions: generic dark SaaS, neon or sci-fi glow, cartoon or mobile-game styling, and gore or splatter horror.",
    "keyCharacteristics": [
      "Real card scans, tokens and rulebook art are the imagery. The interface never competes with them.",
      "Two themes from one token set: the dark table and the light parchment study.",
      "Serif everywhere. Teutonic for names and headings, Alegreya for reading, Georgia for controls.",
      "HUD chrome is grained brown leather with brass edges. The primary action is the one polished brass plate. Glow is a signal, not decoration.",
      "Every animation has a reduced-motion fallback."
    ],
    "rules": [
      {
        "name": "The Earned Glow Rule",
        "body": "Glow means \"act here\". Playable Green rings legal targets and Lamp Glow lights the current step. No other element glows.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Props Rule",
        "body": "Chrome is a physical prop: on the table and over the cover it is grained leather with a lit brass top edge and a downward shadow; on parchment it is card stock. Only the primary action is polished brass, one plate per screen; a switched-on toggle or chosen tab is pressed in, never brass. Content panels like the feed and modals keep Lacquer Black.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Two Inks Rule",
        "body": "Theme switches by redefining the same ink, muted, gold and bad variables. Never hard-code table colors inside the study, or study colors on the table.",
        "section": "colors"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Printed Name Rule",
        "body": "A card, investigator, scenario or encounter name is set in Teutonic, like the printed card. Running text never is.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Both Alphabets Rule",
        "body": "Every display stack lists TeutonicRU right after Teutonic, so Russian names keep the same face.",
        "section": "typography"
      },
      {
        "name": "The Lamplight Rule",
        "body": "Cast shadows are black and fall downward. Colored light exists only as the earned glow. A 1px inset edge in brass or gilt is a bevel on the material, not a shadow.",
        "section": "elevation"
      }
    ],
    "dos": [
      "Do let card scans, tokens and rulebook art carry the color. Interface surfaces stay within Night Table, Smoked Panel, Lacquer Black, the leather and brass of the props, and the parchment tones.",
      "Do set every card, investigator and scenario name in Teutonic with TeutonicRU, and read-at-length text in Alegreya.",
      "Do mark legal targets with the Playable Green ring (3px plus glow) and the current step with Lamp Glow, and nothing else.",
      "Do switch themes by redefining the ink, muted, gold and bad variables, as the parchment and its modals do.",
      "Do give every animation a reduced-motion fallback that places the result instantly.",
      "Do build table chrome as props in CSS: grained leather, a lit brass top edge, a bevel and a shadow that falls downward. Stitch the large plates.",
      "Do keep a single brass plate per screen, for the primary action. Toggles and chosen tabs are pressed in instead.",
      "Do keep every menu screen inside the painted frame: wizard and catalog pad by the frame insets (`clamp(20px, max(6vh, 3.2vw), 64px)` vertical, `clamp(16px, 3.4vw, 56px)` horizontal), and headings stop short of the language tabs."
    ],
    "donts": [
      "Don't use flat grey panels, sans-serif UI or blue accents. Generic dark SaaS styling is rejected.",
      "Don't use neon or sci-fi glow, saturated cyan or purple, glassmorphism or holographic effects.",
      "Don't use cartoon or mobile-game styling: bouncy chunky buttons, bright gradients or loot-box sparkle.",
      "Don't decorate with gore, blood drips, splatter or grunge textures.",
      "Don't add glow, colored cast shadows or pulsing to anything that is not the current step or a legal move.",
      "Don't use pure white or pure black as broad surfaces. Pure black is only for shadows and scrims."
    ]
  }
}
